        ":question/problem": "Given that $g(r,\\theta )={r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{∂g}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r}=2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta }=-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
