        ":question/problem": "Given that $u(r,\\theta )=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂u}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=\\frac{∂u}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $u$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂r}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta }=4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta +4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"
