        ":question/id": "d4815c77-06a6-5342-87f5-965d49f4d616",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-95134",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $u(r,\\theta )=r(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ),$ find $\\frac{∂u}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=\\frac{∂u}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $u$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂r}=2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta }=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      }
